1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,079
At one in the morning, gold miners in the Rocky
Mountains got up and started making

2
00:00:05,129 --> 00:00:05,697
breakfast.

3
00:00:06,033 --> 00:00:09,545
The sky was so bright, they
thought the Sun had risen.

4
00:00:09,867 --> 00:00:15,700
That same night, telegraph wires threw
sparks. Operators got electric shocks.

5
00:00:15,750 --> 00:00:18,680
In some offices, the paper caught fire.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:20,605
It was September 1859.

7
00:00:20,655 --> 00:00:26,624
And the Sun had just hit the Earth with the
biggest storm we have ever recorded.

8
00:00:26,967 --> 00:00:32,023
In 2012, it fired another one just as big.
It missed us by about a week.

9
00:00:32,367 --> 00:00:37,275
This is the story of the storm that already
happened — and what it would do to a

10
00:00:37,325 --> 00:00:39,444
world that now runs on electricity.

11
00:00:39,767 --> 00:00:42,406
On the morning of the first of September,

12
00:00:42,456 --> 00:00:46,865
an English astronomer named Richard
Carrington was drawing sunspots.

13
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:51,531
He pointed his telescope at the Sun and
projected its image onto a sheet of paper —

14
00:00:51,581 --> 00:00:52,956
the safe way to look at it.

15
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:59,580
At eleven eighteen, two beads of blinding
white light burst out of a group of

16
00:00:59,630 --> 00:01:00,320
sunspots.

17
00:01:00,667 --> 00:01:02,044
He ran to find a witness.

18
00:01:02,094 --> 00:01:06,039
By the time he got back, a minute later,
the light was already fading.

19
00:01:06,089 --> 00:01:07,808
Five minutes — and it was gone.

20
00:01:08,133 --> 00:01:11,573
Carrington had seen the first
solar flare in history.

21
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:16,689
What he couldn't see was the cloud it launched:
billions of tonnes of magnetised

22
00:01:16,739 --> 00:01:18,503
gas, flying towards the Earth.

23
00:01:18,833 --> 00:01:22,832
Storms like that usually take two
or three days to cross.

24
00:01:22,882 --> 00:01:25,745
This one took seventeen and a half hours.

25
00:01:26,067 --> 00:01:29,341
When it hit, the Earth's magnetic
field shook like a bell.

26
00:01:29,667 --> 00:01:34,664
Auroras — the northern lights — were seen over
Cuba, over Jamaica, over Hawaii.

27
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:40,551
In the north-eastern United States, people
read their newspapers outside,

28
00:01:40,601 --> 00:01:43,390
at midnight, by the light of the sky.

29
00:01:43,733 --> 00:01:46,161
But the strangest thing
happened on the wires.

30
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:51,716
The storm pushed electric currents into
every long metal line on Earth.

31
00:01:51,766 --> 00:01:56,018
And in 1859, the longest metal lines
were telegraph wires.

32
00:01:56,333 --> 00:02:02,285
Between Boston and Portland, the operators
unplugged their batteries completely —

33
00:02:02,335 --> 00:02:06,731
and kept sending messages for two
hours, powered by the sky.

34
00:02:07,067 --> 00:02:09,591
In 1859 that was a curiosity.

35
00:02:09,641 --> 00:02:14,473
Today, we have wrapped the whole
planet in metal lines.

36
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:18,074
We don't have to guess. Smaller
storms have already shown us.

37
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:23,898
In March 1989, a storm hit Quebec. The power grid
collapsed in about ninety seconds.

38
00:02:23,948 --> 00:02:26,276
Six million people lost electricity.

39
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:32,206
In 1972, a storm set off dozens of American
sea mines off the coast of Vietnam.

40
00:02:32,256 --> 00:02:35,356
Nobody touched them. The
magnetic field did.

41
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:40,880
In 2022, SpaceX launched forty-nine Starlink
satellites into a minor storm.

42
00:02:40,930 --> 00:02:46,458
It puffed up the atmosphere, and dragged
thirty-eight of them back down to burn.

43
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:50,946
And in May 2024, in the middle
of planting season,

44
00:02:50,996 --> 00:02:57,409
farm tractors across the American Midwest
lost their GPS and stopped steering

45
00:02:57,459 --> 00:02:58,332
themselves.

46
00:02:58,666 --> 00:03:01,548
None of these were
Carrington-sized. Not even close.

47
00:03:01,866 --> 00:03:06,360
On the twenty-third of July 2012, the
Sun fired a cloud that was.

48
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:12,317
A NASA spacecraft called STEREO happened to
be in its path, and measured it.

49
00:03:12,367 --> 00:03:15,076
It was as fast as 1859. Maybe faster.

50
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:19,506
It crossed the Earth's orbit where we had
been about a week earlier.

51
00:03:19,833 --> 00:03:25,674
A report for the US National Academies
estimated what a direct hit could cost: up

52
00:03:25,724 --> 00:03:31,275
to two trillion dollars in the first year —
and four to ten years to recover.

53
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:35,037
The weakest point is the giant
transformers that run the grid.

54
00:03:35,087 --> 00:03:37,567
Burn enough of them, and
there are no spares.

55
00:03:37,617 --> 00:03:40,998
Each one is built to order, and takes
a year or more to make.

56
00:03:41,333 --> 00:03:44,001
Today, we're not drawing
sunspots on paper.

57
00:03:44,333 --> 00:03:46,929
Spacecraft watch the Sun all
day, every day.

58
00:03:46,979 --> 00:03:51,859
When a cloud leaves it, we know within hours —
and get one to three days' warning.

59
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:57,643
And a probe parked a million and a half
kilometres towards the Sun gives us the

60
00:03:57,693 --> 00:04:01,746
final alarm: somewhere between
fifteen minutes and an hour.

61
00:04:02,066 --> 00:04:06,000
Enough to put satellites to sleep, reroute
flights away from the poles,

62
00:04:06,050 --> 00:04:07,683
and ease the load on the grid.

63
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,302
So how likely is another Carrington?

64
00:04:10,633 --> 00:04:13,647
One study put it at about twelve
percent per decade.

65
00:04:13,697 --> 00:04:15,769
Later ones say closer to one or two.

66
00:04:16,100 --> 00:04:19,773
Nobody knows the last digit. We
only know it isn't zero.

67
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:22,887
Somewhere on the Sun, the next one
is already waiting.

68
00:04:22,937 --> 00:04:24,516
This time, we'll see it coming.
